<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Qu'aiti Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1858 CE – 1967 CE · capital Mukalla</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The richest of the Hadrami sultanates, founded with money earned in Indian service and ruled from the port of Mukalla.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Qu'aiti were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yafa">Yafa</a>'i family whose head, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-awad-al-quaiti">Umar ibn Awad al-Qu'aiti</a>, rose to high rank as a commander in the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>. With Indian wealth they bought land and influence in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hadramawt">Hadramawt</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibam">Shibam</a> and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ash-shihr">Shihr</a> from around 1858, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukalla">Mukalla</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kasadi-sultanate">Kasadi Sultanate</a> in 1881.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukalla">Mukalla</a> became the capital, with a palace, customs house and modern administration; the sultans long spent much time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a>. Treaties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> from 1882 made the state part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aden-protectorate">Aden Protectorate</a>, later the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden-protectorate">Aden Protectorate</a>, and the Kathiri-Qu'aiti agreement of 1918 settled relations with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kathiri-sultanate">Kathiri Sultanate</a>. Remittances from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadhrami-bedouin-legion">Hadhrami Bedouin Legion</a> sustained the state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In September 1967 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukalla">Mukalla</a> while the young sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghalib-ibn-awad-al-quaiti">Ghalib ibn Awad al-Qu'aiti</a> was abroad, and the sultanate was abolished within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/quaiti-sultanate.jpg" alt="Qu'aiti Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: ArnoldPlaton and 痛Designism · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Qu%27aiti_Sultanate_(1939-1967).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1858 CE – 1967 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mukalla</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Umar ibn Awad al-Qu'aiti, Ghalib ibn Awad al-Qu'aiti</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qu'aiti State of Shihr and Mukalla, Quaiti Sultanate, Qu'aiti</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1912&amp;focus=quaiti-sultanate" aria-label="Qu'aiti Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6024 1637 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Qu'aiti Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6344 1893L6344 1893M6344 1893L6295 1853M6344 1893L6236 1957"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6344 1893h0M6295 1853h0M6236 1957h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6344 1893h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6344 1893h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mukalla. <a href="/atlas?year=1912&amp;focus=quaiti-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
